-- title:
--   Čerednik–Drinfel'd embedding of ̄ F into K(mathcal X_{0,C})
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $C$ denotes the completion `A₂.valuation.Completion` of the algebraic closure $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ at the valuation subring $A_2$, and $\kappa$ denotes [`ValuationSubring.ratClosure A₂`](def/ValuationSubring_CompletionRatClosure.html#L13), the topological closure of the prime subfield inside $C$. For a scheme over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O_0$ the subscript $C$ denotes the base change along $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the composite $\mathcal O_0 \to \kappa \to C$; thus $\mathcal X_{0,C}$ is the fibre product of `f₀` with that morphism, and $\mathcal Y_{\ell,C}$ the fibre product of `g₀ ℓ`. Finally $\iota_C : C \to K(\mathcal X_{0,C})$ denotes [`AlgebraicCurve.baseToFunctionField`](def/AlgebraicCurve_CurveModel.html#L18) of the second projection of $\mathcal X_{0,C}$, that is, the map on function fields induced by the structure morphism to $\operatorname{Spec} C$.
--
--   *Arithmetic data.* Natural numbers $N \neq 0$ squarefree, primes $q, q' \geq 5$ with $q' \neq q$, neither dividing $N$, and a nonzero $D$ divisible by $2Nqq'$. A valuation subring $A_2$ of $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ with `hA₂ : A₂.LiesOverPrime q`, i.e. $q$ lies in the non-units of $A_2$; the hypothesis `hiso₂` records that every element of the decomposition subgroup of $A_2$ over $\mathbb Q$ preserves the valuation. A finite place $v_2$ of $\mathbb Q$ with $q \in v_2$.
--
--   *Definite side.* Rationals $a_1, b_1$ with `hdef₁`: $a_1 < 0$, $b_1 < 0$, and for each finite place $v$ the algebra $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1] \otimes_{\mathbb Q} \mathbb Q_v$ is a division algebra exactly when $q' \in v$. Submodules $\Lambda_1, R_1$ with $\Lambda_1$ a maximal order, $R_1$ an Eichler order of level $N$ (an intersection of two maximal orders of relative index $N$ in the first), and $R_1 \leq \Lambda_1$. A finite-idelic unit $n_1$ lying in `primeHeckeSet R₁ q`, such that `meetOrder R₁ n₁` $= R_1 \cap R_1^{n_1}$ is an Eichler order of level $Nq$ (`hS₁`), is normalised by $n_1$ (`hnorm₁`), and such that shifting twice by $n_1$ on the class set of its finite-idelic stabiliser is the identity (`hsq₁`). The hypothesis `hlaws₁ : ClassSetHeckeLaws N q Λ₁ R₁ n₁` comprises four clauses: the edge Hecke matrices commute pairwise, the vertex Hecke matrices commute pairwise, the two degeneracy push-forwards intertwine edge and vertex Hecke operators at primes other than $q$, and the joint kernel of the degeneracy maps is stable under the edge Hecke operators.
--
--   *Indefinite side and the model over $\bar{\mathbb Q}$.* Rationals $a, b$ with `hB`: $0 < a$ or $0 < b$, and a finite place $v$ ramifies exactly when $q \in v$ or $q' \in v$; a maximal order $\Lambda$. A field $\bar F$ over $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ which is a curve over $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ in the sense of `IsCurveOver` (principal divisors of degree zero for every nonzero element, residue fields of all places finite over $\bar{\mathbb Q}$, and $\Omega_{\bar F/\bar{\mathbb Q}}$ free of rank one) and essentially of finite type over $\bar{\mathbb Q}$. A scheme $X$ with $\pi_X : X \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z[1/D]$, a point $\bar s : \operatorname{Spec}\bar{\mathbb Q} \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z[1/D]$, and an assignment `pt` of $\pi_X$-points to fake elliptic curves of level $N$ for $\Lambda$, satisfying the four conditions `pt_iso`, `pt_pullback`, `pt_surjective`, `pt_injective` (invariance under isomorphism, compatibility with base change of the ring, and surjectivity and injectivity up to isomorphism on points valued in algebraically closed fields); the universal property of a coarse moduli space is not assumed for $X$. A curve model $\mathfrak M$ of $\bar F$ over $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ together with an isomorphism $e_{\mathfrak M} : \mathfrak M.C \to X \times_{\mathbb Z[1/D]} \bar{\mathbb Q}$ compatible with the structure morphisms (`he𝔐`). A homomorphism `gal` from $\operatorname{Aut}(\bar{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ to the semilinear automorphisms of $\bar F$, tower data $\mathbb T$ of level structures at the primes $\ell \neq q, q'$ with Galois actions `galT` on each $\mathbb T.F\,\ell$, two semilinear automorphisms $W_0, W_1$ of $\bar F$ and their tower analogues `WT`, and a witness `tw : ModuliTowerWitnessD Λ N q q' D Fbar X πX sbar pt 𝔐 e𝔐 gal 𝕋 galT W WT` tying the moduli interpretation of $X$ and of the tower to places of $\bar F$ and of the $\mathbb T.F\,\ell$.
--
--   *Local data at $q$.* An injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra map $\iota_2$ of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ into $2\times 2$ matrices over $\kappa$ and the induced projective representation $\rho_2$ into $\mathrm{PGL}_2(\kappa)$ (`hρ₂`); a pseudo-uniformizer $\varpi_2$ whose image in $C$ is $q$ (`hϖ₂`), with `hdom₂` asserting that the holomorphic ring `Omega.HolRingOf ϖ₂ ρ₂` is a domain. Families $s_2$ and $sf_2$ indexed by the primes $\ell \neq q, q'$, with `hs₂` (four clauses per $\ell$): $sf_2(\ell)$ has component $s_2(\ell) \otimes 1$ at every finite place not containing $q$ and component $1$ at the places containing $q$; the product of the diagonal idele of the scalar $\ell$ with $sf_2(\ell)^{-1}$ lies in `levelHeckeUSet Λ₁ (meetOrder R₁ n₁) ℓ` when $\ell \mid N$ and in `primeHeckeSet (meetOrder R₁ n₁) ℓ` otherwise; and $\mathrm{nrd}(s_2(\ell)) = \ell$. A family of subgroups $\Gamma_2$ indexed by `Option` of those primes, with `hΓ₂0` describing $\Gamma_2(\text{none})$ as the units lying in `CosetGraph.awayUnits R₁ v₂` whose reduced norm has even $q$-adic valuation, and `hΓ₂ℓ` describing $\Gamma_2(\ell)$ as the intersection of $\Gamma_2(\text{none})$ with its conjugate by $s_2(\ell)$. Families $w_2$ and $\bar w_2$ with `hw₂`: $w_2(\text{none})$ lies in `awayUnits R₁ v₂` and has reduced norm $q$, and $w_2(\ell)$ lies in `awayUnits (meetOrder R₁ (sf₂ ℓ)) v₂` with reduced norm $q$; and `hwbar₂`: $\bar w_2(\text{none})$ has reduced norm $q'$, its local image at every place $u \neq v_2$ not containing $q'$ lies in the local box units of $R_1$, and conjugation by it preserves the local boxes of $R_1$ and of $\Lambda_1$ at every $u \neq v_2$, with the same three conditions for each $\bar w_2(\ell)$, the order $R_1$ being replaced by `meetOrder R₁ (sf₂ ℓ)`. A homomorphism $dIso_2$ from the decomposition subgroup of $A_2$ to the isometric automorphisms of $C$ over $\kappa$, inducing the given action (`hdIso₂`). A quadratic character $\chi$ of the decomposition subgroup, trivial on inertia (`hχ₁`), nontrivial on any Frobenius at $q$ (`hχ₂`), and with kernel exactly the elements fixing every $x$ in the residue field of $A_2$ with $x^{q^2} = x$ (`hχ₃`).
--
--   *Integral data and the uniformisation.* A characteristic-zero discrete valuation ring $\mathcal O_0$ with irreducible $\pi$, $\pi$-adically complete, residue field of cardinality $q$, and $(q) = (\pi)$, realised as a subring of $\kappa$ with $\kappa$ as fraction field; a characteristic-zero $\mathcal O_0$-domain $Onr$ with an $\mathcal O_0$-automorphism $Fr$, complete and local at $\pi Onr$ with $\pi Onr$ maximal, integral over $\mathcal O_0$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOa`), such that every monic polynomial of positive degree has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOcl`), and with $Fr(x) \equiv x^q$ modulo $\pi$ (`hFr`). A homomorphism $vdet$ on $\mathrm{GL}_2(\kappa)$ measuring the $\pi$-valuation of the determinant (`hvdet`), a ring map $\psi_0 : Onr \to C$ with $\pi \mapsto \varpi_2$ (`hπϖ`), $N$ invertible in $\mathcal O_0$ (`hN₀`), the identification of $\mathcal O_0$ with the elements of $\kappa$ of valuation at most one (`h𝒪₀`), the bound `hψ₀` on the valuations of $\psi_0$, compatibility `hψ₀𝒪` of $\psi_0$ with the structure maps, and the rank-one property `hrk` of the valuation on $C$. An integral scheme $\mathcal X_0$ with a flat, separated, locally of finite type, quasi-compact morphism $f_0$ to $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O_0$ and a moduli assignment $pt_0$ making $(\mathcal X_0, f_0, pt_0)$ a coarse moduli space (`h𝒳₀`, including the universal property), together with coarse moduli spaces $(\mathcal Y_0(\ell), g_0(\ell), ptT_0(\ell))$ for the problem with extra level $\ell$; degeneracy morphisms $d_{0}(\ell), d_{1}(\ell) : \mathcal Y_0(\ell) \to \mathcal X_0$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O_0$, the first inducing the forgetting of the extra level and the second the passage to the quotient by a level-$\ell$ isogeny on moduli points; and endomorphisms $ar, \overline{ar}$ of $\mathcal X_0$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O_0$ inducing the Atkin–Lehner quotients at $q$ and at $q'$ on moduli points. Natural maps $\Theta$ and $\Theta_T(\ell)$ from the functor of pairs (an $\mathcal O_0$-algebra map $Onr \to B$, a Deligne datum over $B$) on $\mathcal O_0$-algebras $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent, to the $B$-points of $f_0$ and of $g_0(\ell)$. The hypothesis `hCD` is a conjunction of twelve clauses, summarised here: naturality of $\Theta$ and of each $\Theta_T(\ell)$; invariance of $\Theta$ under the twisted action (through $\iota_2$, $Fr$ and $vdet$) of the units in `awayUnits R₁ v₂`, and of $\Theta_T(\ell)$ under that of the intersection of this group with its $s_2(\ell)$-conjugate; over algebraically closed fields with $\pi$ nilpotent, surjectivity of $\Theta$ and $\Theta_T(\ell)$ with fibres exactly the orbits of those groups; the initiality of $\Theta$ and of each $\Theta_T(\ell)$ among invariant natural transformations into the nilpotent points of an arbitrary scheme over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O_0$; the compatibility of $d_0(\ell)$ with $\Theta_T(\ell)$ and $\Theta$, and of $d_1(\ell)$ with the twisted action of $s_2(\ell)$; and the identification of $ar$ with the Frobenius twist by $1$ of the $Onr$-component and of $\overline{ar}$ with the twisted action of $\bar w_2(\text{none})$.
--
--   *Comparison over the completion.* The base changes $\mathcal X_{0,C}$ and every $\mathcal Y_{\ell,C}$ are assumed integral. Finally, an isomorphism $u$ between the pullback of $\pi_X$ along $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the map $\bar{\mathbb Q} \to C$ composed with $\bar s$, and $\mathcal X_{0,C}$, compatible with the projections to $\operatorname{Spec} C$ (`hu`) and with the two moduli assignments `pt` and `pt₀` on points valued in $C$-algebras (`hupt`).
--
--   Under these hypotheses there exists a ring homomorphism $j : \bar F \to K(\mathcal X_{0,C})$ with the following six properties.
--
--   First, $j$ is compatible with the base: for every $z \in \bar{\mathbb Q}$, $j$ of the image of $z$ in $\bar F$ equals $\iota_C$ of the image of $z$ in $C$.
--
--   Second, $j$ generates: the subfield of $K(\mathcal X_{0,C})$ generated by the union of the image of $\iota_C$ and the image of $j$ is everything.
--
--   Third, $j$ is linearly disjoint from the base: for every finite subset $t$ of $\bar F$ whose elements are linearly independent over $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ and every family of coefficients $c_x \in C$ indexed by $t$, the relation $\sum_{x \in t} \iota_C(c_x)\, j(x) = 0$ forces $c_x = 0$ for all $x \in t$.
--
--   Fourth, $j$ intertwines the Atkin–Lehner endomorphisms with $W_0$ and $W_1$: for every endomorphism $a$ of $\mathcal X_{0,C}$ such that $a$ followed by the first projection equals the first projection followed by $ar$, and $a$ followed by the second projection equals the second projection, and for every $x \in \bar F$, every open $U$ of $\mathcal X_{0,C}$ containing the generic point, with the generic point also lying in $a^{-1}U$, and every section $\mathrm{sec}$ over $U$ whose germ at the generic point is $j(x)$, the germ at the generic point of the pullback of $\mathrm{sec}$ along $a$ over $a^{-1}U$ equals $j(W_0 \cdot x)$. The same statement holds with $ar$ replaced by $\overline{ar}$ and $W_0$ by $W_1$.
--
--   Fifth, $j$ is equivariant for the decomposition group: for every $\tau$ in the decomposition subgroup of $A_2$ over $\mathbb Q$ and every endomorphism $t_C$ of $\mathcal X_{0,C}$ with $t_C$ followed by the first projection equal to the first projection, and $t_C$ followed by the second projection equal to the second projection followed by $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the isometric automorphism $dIso_2(\tau)$ of $C$, the germ computation above yields, for $x \in \bar F$ and a section with germ $j(x)$, the value $j(\mathrm{gal}(\tau) \cdot x)$, where $\tau$ is regarded as an automorphism of $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ over $\mathbb Q$.
--
--   Sixth, $j$ extends to the tower: for every prime $\ell \neq q, q'$ there exists a ring homomorphism $j_\ell : \mathbb T.F\,\ell \to K(\mathcal Y_{\ell,C})$ such that for each $k \in \{0,1\}$ and each morphism $d_C : \mathcal Y_{\ell,C} \to \mathcal X_{0,C}$ with $d_C$ followed by the first projection equal to the first projection of $\mathcal Y_{\ell,C}$ followed by $d_0(\ell)$ when $k = 0$ and by $d_1(\ell)$ when $k = 1$, and $d_C$ followed by the second projection equal to the second projection, the following holds: for every $x \in \bar F$, every open $U$ of $\mathcal X_{0,C}$ containing its generic point such that the generic point of $\mathcal Y_{\ell,C}$ lies in $d_C^{-1}U$, and every section over $U$ whose germ at the generic point is $j(x)$, the germ at the generic point of $\mathcal Y_{\ell,C}$ of the pullback of that section along $d_C$ equals $j_\ell(\mathbb T.\varphi(\ell,k)(x))$.
--
--   This is the transport step of the Čerednik–Drinfel'd comparison: the $\bar{\mathbb Q}$-model of the Shimura curve attached to the indefinite quaternion algebra ramified exactly at $q$ and $q'$, together with its Hecke tower, its Atkin–Lehner involutions and its Galois action, is carried into the function field of the base change to the completion $C$ of the $p$-adically uniformised integral model $\mathcal X_0$ over $\mathcal O_0$. The embedding $j$ produced here, with its generation, linear disjointness and equivariance properties, is what is used by [`CerednikDrinfeld.exists_ringHom_functionField_pullback_completion_of_moduliTowerWitness_zero_one_of_two_mul_dvd`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.exists_ringHom_functionField_pullback_completion_of_moduliTowerWitness_zero_one_of_two_mul_dvd).
